  "id": "raramuri_ritual_fiesta_music",
  "name": "Rarámuri (Tarahumara) ritual and fiesta music",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Music of the Rarámuri of the Sierra Tarahumara in Chihuahua. In the yúmari and tutuguri ceremonies a chanter sings while shaking a gourd rattle as people dance for rain, crops and the dead; at Holy Week, groups of fariseos and soldados process to the steady beat of large double-headed drums with cane flutes; and at fiestas such as the matachín dances, locally made violins and guitars play repeated dance tunes. The INAH Testimonio Musical de México series documents the music. Generic drum, flute and shaker cards stand in for the Rarámuri drum, cane flute and gourd rattle.",
  "recipe": "classic modern rarámuri (tarahumara) ritual and fiesta music, ritual Devotional, indigenous branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. fiddle, parlor acoustic guitar, membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum, flute like credited flute shaker source unspecified. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
